window.__labLoaded({"id":"1.2","title":"Range, Quartiles and the Box Plot","goal":"Find the range, quartiles, IQR and outlier fences for the lesson's ten waiting times and draw one box plot.","question":"A clinic manager has the waiting times (in minutes) of ten patients. The average hides how long the unlucky patients waited. She wants the spread, the range of the middle 50% of patients, and a fair test of whether the longest wait of 90 minutes is truly unusual.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Waiting times and the range","text":"The lesson's nine waiting times in minutes plus one patient who waited 90. The range is the largest minus the smallest value, so it only uses two numbers.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nwait = np.array([8, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 31, 38, 45, 90])\n\nprint('n =', len(wait))\nprint('min, max =', wait.min(), wait.max())\nprint('range =', wait.max() - wait.min())","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, as np.","Load Matplotlib's pyplot as plt; we use it for the box plot in the last step.","Store the ten waiting times, sorted. The last patient waited 90 minutes.","Print how many patients we have, so we know the sample size.","Print the shortest and longest waits, to see the two extremes.","Range = largest minus smallest. It is easy but sees only two patients, so treat it as a first look."],"meaning":"There are ten patients. The shortest wait was 8 minutes and the longest 90, so the range is 82 minutes. The range only uses two patients, so one very long wait inflates it. We need measures that look at the middle of the data.","out":"n = 10\nmin, max = 8 90\nrange = 82","figs":[]},{"title":"Quartiles and IQR, by the lesson's recipe","text":"With 10 values, split the sorted data into a lower half and an upper half of 5. Q1 and Q3 are the medians of those halves. The IQR is the width of the middle 50%.","code":"lower, upper = wait[:5], wait[5:]\nq1, q2, q3 = np.median(lower), np.median(wait), np.median(upper)\niqr = q3 - q1\n\nprint('Q1, median, Q3 =', q1, q2, q3)\nprint('IQR =', iqr)","explain":["Split the sorted data into a lower half (first five) and an upper half (last five). Slicing wait[:5] takes the first five values.","Q1 is the median of the lower half, Q2 the median of everything and Q3 the median of the upper half: the three quartiles.","The IQR (Q3 minus Q1) is the width of the middle 50%. It ignores both extremes, so one odd value cannot wreck it.","Print the three quartiles together.","Print the IQR."],"meaning":"Q1 = 15, median = 24.5 and Q3 = 38. A quarter of patients waited 15 minutes or less, and a quarter waited 38 minutes or more. The IQR is 23 minutes, so the middle half of patients differ by only 23 minutes. That is much smaller than the range of 82.","out":"Q1, median, Q3 = 15.0 24.5 38.0\nIQR = 23.0","figs":[]},{"title":"Fences and outliers","text":"Tukey's rule marks anything below Q1 - 1.5 x IQR or above Q3 + 1.5 x IQR as an outlier. Flagged does not mean wrong: it means look at it.","code":"lower_fence = q1 - 1.5 * iqr\nupper_fence = q3 + 1.5 * iqr\n\nprint('fences:', lower_fence, 'and', upper_fence)\nprint('outliers:', wait[(wait < lower_fence) | (wait > upper_fence)])\n\nsoftware = np.percentile(wait, [25, 75])\nprint('NumPy default Q1, Q3 =', software, '(a different, also valid, method)')","explain":["Tukey's lower fence sits 1.5 IQRs below Q1. A value beyond it would be flagged as unusually low.","The upper fence sits 1.5 IQRs above Q3. A value beyond it is flagged as unusually high.","Print both fences.","Select the values below the lower fence or above the upper fence. The | symbol means 'or'.","Compute the quartiles with NumPy's built-in method, to compare with our hand method.","Print them, noting that another method gives slightly different but equally valid quartiles."],"meaning":"The fences are -19.5 and 72.5, so only the 90-minute wait lies outside and counts as an outlier. A lower fence below 0 can never catch anything here. NumPy's own method gives Q1 = 15.75 and Q3 = 36.25, a little different, which is fine. Flagged does not mean wrong: it means look into it.","out":"fences: -19.5 and 72.5\noutliers: [90]\nNumPy default Q1, Q3 = [15.75 36.25] (a different, also valid, method)","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the box plot","text":"The box runs from Q1 to Q3 with a line at the median. Whiskers stop at the most extreme values inside the fences, and the dot at 90 is the outlier. Matplotlib uses NumPy's quartile method, so its box edges are 15.75 and 36.25.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.boxplot(wait, vert=False)\nplt.xlabel('waiting time (minutes)')\nplt.yticks([])\nplt.title('Waiting times with one long wait')\nplt.show()","explain":["Open a new blank figure, 6 by 3.4 inches, a size that fits the page well.","Draw the box plot lying down (vert=False), so that minutes run left to right.","Label the horizontal axis with the unit, so the reader knows what the numbers are.","Remove the vertical tick labels, which mean nothing because there is only one box.","Add a title that says what the picture shows.","Show the figure."],"meaning":"The box covers the middle half of waits (about 16 to 36 minutes) and the line inside it is the median. The left whisker reaches 8 and the right whisker reaches 45, the largest value inside the fences. The lone dot at 90 is the outlier. The picture matches the numbers: a tight bunch of waits and one very long one.","out":"","figs":["1.2-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"wait <- c(8, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 31, 38, 45, 90)\ndiff(range(wait))\nfivenum(wait)\nquantile(wait, c(0.25, 0.75))\nIQR(wait)\nboxplot(wait, horizontal = TRUE)","explain":["Store the ten waiting times in a vector.","range() returns the minimum and maximum, and diff() subtracts them to give the range.","fivenum() gives Tukey's five numbers: minimum, lower hinge (Q1), median, upper hinge (Q3) and maximum.","quantile() gives the quartiles by R's default method, which differs slightly from fivenum with small data.","IQR() gives the width of the middle half by that default method.","boxplot() draws the box plot, and horizontal = TRUE lays it on its side."],"expect":"range 82; fivenum 8, 15, 24.5, 38, 90; quantile 25% 15.75, 75% 36.25; IQR 20.5; the box plot shows 90 as an outlier","read":"diff(range(wait)) prints 82, and fivenum prints 8, 15, 24.5, 38, 90. quantile prints 25% 15.75 and 75% 36.25, and IQR prints 20.5. The box plot shows 90 as a dot beyond the whisker. Two quartile methods differ a little with only ten values, so say which one you used.","note":"fivenum() uses the lesson's hand recipe (Q1 = 15, Q3 = 38). quantile() and IQR() use R's default method, which equals NumPy's default (15.75 and 36.25, IQR 20.5). boxplot() draws the hand-recipe box."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / wait.\nBEGIN DATA\n8  12  15  18  22  27  31  38  45  90\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=wait\n  /PLOT=BOXPLOT\n  /STATISTICS=DESCRIPTIVES\n  /PERCENTILES(25,50,75).","explain":["Tell SPSS we type the data in the syntax, in free format, into one variable called wait.","BEGIN DATA starts the data lines.","The ten waiting times, separated by spaces.","END DATA ends the data lines.","EXAMINE is SPSS's Explore command, which gives detailed descriptives and charts.","Ask for a box plot of the waiting times.","Include the Descriptives table, which has the range among other things.","Ask for the 25th, 50th and 75th percentiles, which are Q1, the median and Q3."],"read":"In the Descriptives table read Range 82.00 and Median 24.50. The Percentiles table has a Tukey's Hinges row (15, 24.5, 38, the hand method of the lesson) and a Weighted Average row, a slightly different method. The box plot marks the 90 as an outlier.","note":"The Percentiles table has two rows: Tukey's Hinges (the lesson's recipe: 15, 24.5, 38) and Weighted Average (another valid method, a little different). SPSS box plots mark 90 as an outlier."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 10\ngenerate wait = real(word(\"8  12  15  18  22  27  31  38  45  90\", _n))\nsummarize wait, detail\ndisplay r(p75) - r(p25)\ngraph hbox wait","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Create ten empty rows.","Fill wait row by row: word() picks the n-th number and real() makes it numeric.","summarize with detail lists the percentiles (25%, 50%, 75%), the smallest and largest values and more.","summarize leaves its results in memory as r(). Subtracting p25 from p75 gives the IQR.","Draw a horizontal box plot of the waiting times."],"read":"In the summarize table the 25%, 50% and 75% rows are 15, 24.5 and 38, and the smallest and largest values are 8 and 90. The display line prints 23, the IQR. The box plot shows 90 as a dot beyond the whisker.","note":"The word() line is a compact way to type the data. summarize, detail lists p25 = 15, p50 = 24.5 and p75 = 38 (the same as the lesson's recipe for these data), so the display line prints the IQR of 23. graph hbox draws the box plot."},"conclusion":"A typical wait is about 24.5 minutes (the median). The middle half of patients waited between 15 and 38 minutes (IQR 23), and the 90-minute wait is the one outlier by the 1.5 x IQR rule, since the upper fence is 72.5. The range of 82 is mostly that one patient. Caution: flagged is not wrong, so check whether it was a real long wait or a recording error before removing it.","practice":{"scenario":"A pizza shop times 12 deliveries, in minutes from order to door. The owner wants to know how reliable delivery is and whether the 71-minute delivery was a freak or normal variation.","data":"pizza = [18, 22, 24, 25, 27, 28, 30, 31, 33, 35, 38, 71]","tasks":["Find the range of the delivery times.","Find Q1, the median, Q3 and the IQR (np.percentile is fine).","Compute the 1.5 x IQR fences and name any outlier.","Find the range again without the outlier."],"hint":"np.percentile(data, [25, 50, 75]) gives the three quartiles. Then fences are Q1 - 1.5 x IQR and Q3 + 1.5 x IQR.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\n\npizza = np.array([18, 22, 24, 25, 27, 28, 30, 31, 33, 35, 38, 71])\nq1, q2, q3 = np.percentile(pizza, [25, 50, 75])\niqr = q3 - q1\nlow, high = q1 - 1.5 * iqr, q3 + 1.5 * iqr\nprint('range =', pizza.max() - pizza.min())\nprint(f'Q1 = {q1}, median = {q2}, Q3 = {q3}, IQR = {iqr}')\nprint(f'fences: {low} to {high}')\nprint('outliers:', pizza[(pizza < low) | (pizza > high)])\nprint('range without outlier =', pizza[pizza <= high].max() - pizza.min())","answer":"The range is 53 minutes. With NumPy's quartile method Q1 = 24.75, the median is 29.0, Q3 = 33.5 and the IQR is 8.75, so the fences are 11.625 and 46.625. Only 71 lies outside, so it is an outlier. Without it the range is just 20 minutes. Most deliveries take 25 to 34 minutes, so find out what went wrong with the 71.","out":"range = 53\nQ1 = 24.75, median = 29.0, Q3 = 33.5, IQR = 8.75\nfences: 11.625 to 46.625\noutliers: [71]\nrange without outlier = 20","figs":[]}}});
